  "preview": "Three-act task. The 7th grade is filling 500 water balloons for Field Day. The video shows the first 30 seconds at one spigot. How long is this going to take?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use a rate observed in a short video clip to predict how long a much larger job will take. Reason about the precision of an estimate, then revise the estimate when new information arrives.",
    "studentPrompt": "Field Day is tomorrow. The 7th grade team needs 500 filled water balloons by lunchtime. Mr. Alvarez is filling them at the spigot outside the gym.\n\nIn the first 30 seconds, he fills 7 balloons. Every 25 balloons, he has to stop for 45 seconds to untangle the next bundle and re-tie the spigot. There are two more spigots on the wall — two other teachers can each fill at the same rate as Mr. Alvarez, with the same 45-second break every 25 balloons.\n\n1. Make an estimate first. Working alone at this pace, how long will Mr. Alvarez take to fill all 500 balloons? Write down a too-high guess, a too-low guess, then your best estimate. (Don't compute yet — just feel it out.)\n2. Now figure it out. Working alone, how long will Mr. Alvarez actually take? Give your answer in minutes and seconds. How does it compare to your estimate?\n3. With all three teachers filling at once, how long will it take?\n4. Mr. Alvarez actually started at 8:42 AM and finished the last balloon at 9:14 AM, working with both other teachers. How close was your Q3 prediction? What might explain any difference?\n5. The 8th grade wants 800 balloons for their Field Day. They have four spigots and four teachers; their spigot fills 9 balloons every 30 seconds (newer hardware), with the same 45-second break every 25 balloons. Will the 8th grade finish faster or slower than the 7th grade did? Predict first, then compute.",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/34589651/pexels-photo-34589651.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Vibrant balloons tied by the ocean, creating a serene sunset scene.",
      "credit": "Photo by Kerim Gökali on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the Act 1 image (or, if you have time, mime the scene). Pause. 'Mr. Alvarez fills 7 balloons in 30 seconds. They need 500 balloons. How long is this going to take?' Have every student write a too-high, a too-low, and a best guess on a sticky note before any math happens. Collect a few estimates on the board. THEN release Act 2 info.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Sticky notes for Act 1 estimates",
      "Optional: timer or clock for the 'feel' of the rate"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "unit-rate",
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "estimation",
      "rate-times-time",
      "three-act-task"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "A classic 3-act structure pulled into BTC. Low floor (every student can write a too-high/too-low guess). The break-every-25-balloons twist is the diamond — it changes the structure of the calculation from 'pure rate' to 'rate + overhead'. The sequel keeps strong groups busy.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Act 1: Estimates will vary wildly. Lock them in before Act 2.\n- Q1 (one person, no breaks): rate = 7 balloons / 30 sec, so 500 balloons takes (500/7) × 30 ≈ 2142.9 sec ≈ 35 min 43 sec.\n- Q1 (with breaks): 500 ÷ 25 = 20 bundles, but the last bundle doesn't need a re-tie after it. So 19 breaks × 45 sec = 855 sec ≈ 14 min 15 sec of breaks. Total ≈ 2142.9 + 855 ≈ 2998 sec ≈ 49 min 58 sec.\n- Q3 (three teachers): If they perfectly split the work, ~500/3 ≈ 167 balloons each. Filling time per person: (167/7)×30 ≈ 715 sec. Breaks per person: each fills ~6 bundles (or 7), so 6 breaks × 45 = 270 sec. Per person ≈ 985 sec ≈ 16 min 25 sec. So they all finish in about 16-17 minutes. Strong groups may argue about whether 167 splits evenly into 25s — celebrate that argument.\n- Act 3 reveal: 8:42 to 9:14 = 32 minutes. Their model predicted ~16-17 min. Why the gap? (Setup, talking, water pressure dropping, jokes, refilling tubs, etc.)",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting the 45-second breaks entirely and reporting only the 'pure fill time'.\n- Counting a break after the LAST bundle (there are 20 bundles but only 19 breaks between them, and arguably zero after the last).\n- Dividing the total TIME by 3 to get the 3-person answer instead of dividing the WORK by 3 and recomputing breaks per person.\n- Sloppy unit work: getting an answer like '714 minutes' because they multiplied by 30 instead of dividing.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Whose Act 1 guess was closest? What did they 'feel' about the rate that helped?\n- Why is the three-teacher time NOT exactly one-third of the one-teacher time?\n- The reveal said 32 minutes. Our model said about 17. What does the model leave out? When is that OK, and when does it matter?\n- If you were the principal, would you trust this model to plan NEXT year's Field Day? Why or why not?",
    "extensions": "- Sequel (8th grade): rate = 9/30 = 0.3 balloons/sec, so 800 balloons takes 800/0.3 ≈ 2667 sec for one person. With 4 people: 200 balloons each → (200/9)×30 ≈ 667 sec of filling + (200/25 − 1) = 7 breaks × 45 = 315 sec → ~982 sec ≈ 16 min 22 sec per person. Roughly the same as 7th grade, even though they have more balloons and more spigots. Discuss why.\n- A bottleneck question: what if there are only TWO working spigots but three teachers? One teacher has to wait. Now what's the fastest possible time?\n- Reverse question: the 6th grade wants to finish in EXACTLY 20 minutes flat. How many spigots and teachers do they need?",
    "sampleSolutions": "Rate: 7 balloons / 30 sec = 0.2333... balloons/sec, or equivalently 30/7 ≈ 4.29 sec/balloon.\n\nQ1 (one person, with breaks):\n• Fill time: 500 balloons × (30/7) sec ≈ 2142.9 sec.\n• Breaks: 500/25 = 20 bundles, so 19 break gaps × 45 sec = 855 sec.\n• Total ≈ 2998 sec ≈ 49 min 58 sec.\n\nQ3 (three people, parallel):\n• Each fills ~500/3 ≈ 167 balloons.\n• Fill time per person: 167 × (30/7) ≈ 715 sec.\n• Breaks per person: ~6 break gaps × 45 = 270 sec.\n• Each person finishes in ≈ 985 sec ≈ 16 min 25 sec.\n• Since they work in parallel, the whole job takes about 16-17 minutes.\n\nAct 3: actual time = 32 minutes. Our model predicted ~17 min — the difference comes from unmodeled overhead (setup, dropped balloons, tub swaps, conversation). The model is a LOWER BOUND on the real time.\n\nSequel: 8th grade ≈ 16-17 min per person × 4 in parallel. Surprisingly, faster hardware + more workers roughly cancels out the larger job.",
